# park.fan frontend environment
# Copy this file to .env.local and fill in what you need; nothing is required for
# `pnpm dev`. Every variable the code reads, with its default and its reader, is in
# docs/development/setup.md#environment-variables.

# Backend API auth key — sent as the `x-auth-key` header on every server-side
# request to api.park.fan. Server-only (NOT NEXT_PUBLIC_): the key is never
# exposed to the browser; client requests go through the Next.js proxy routes,
# or without the key straight to the API (NEXT_PUBLIC_API_DIRECT below).
# API_AUTH_KEY=

# Backend API origin for server-side requests and for the browser's direct calls.
# Unset → https://api.park.fan. NEXT_PUBLIC_, so it is inlined at build time:
# change it, rebuild.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL=https://api.park.fan

# "1" → the browser calls the API at NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL directly for the
# per-visitor requests no cache can share (nearby, favorites, trips, push), so
# they never start a Vercel function. Any other value or unset → the /api relay
# routes. Needs the API's CORS list to name this origin. See
# docs/rules/a-request-no-cache-can-share-goes-to-the-api-directly.md.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_API_DIRECT=1

# Origin the OG image URLs are built on (lib/utils/og-image.ts) and the OG renderer
# falls back to for its pictures. Unset → https://park.fan. Every other absolute URL
# uses the SITE_URL constant in i18n/config.ts. NEXT_PUBLIC_, so rebuild after a change.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_BASE_URL=https://park.fan

# Umami Analytics, switched off: the layout embeds no tracker, so nothing reads these until it is
# switched back on (docs/development/analytics.md).
# Tracker script URL
NEXT_PUBLIC_UMAMI_URL=https://cloud.umami.is/script.js

# Your Umami Website ID from the dashboard
NEXT_PUBLIC_UMAMI_WEBSITE_ID=563fb5d1-2006-4e93-b2bd-439869690ebe


# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# CARTO — basemap tiles for the park map (parks/park-map.tsx) and the admin
# parks catalogue map (admin/parks/_components/parks-map.tsx)
# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Raises the request ceiling above CARTO's anonymous fair-use tier — see
# docs/rules/map-tiles-are-carto-not-osms-own-tile-server.md for why we don't
# hotlink OpenStreetMap's own tile server instead. Get a key at
# https://carto.com (Basemaps). Unset → falls back to the anonymous
# subdomain-sharded endpoint, fine for local dev.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_CARTO_MAP_KEY=

# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Cloudflare Turnstile — bot protection
# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# One widget, two surfaces: the upload form (verified once in
# /api/contribute/start) and the ADMIN LOGIN (verified in /api/admin/session,
# before the credentials are forwarded to api.park.fan at all).
# Get the keys at https://dash.cloudflare.com → Turnstile.
# Public site key (browser widget) — safe to commit, it ships in the HTML.
# park.fan's widget:
# NEXT_PUBLIC_TURNSTILE_SITE_KEY=0x4AAAAAADtYQ5UB5mmVClFJ
# Left commented on purpose: unset, the forms fall back to Cloudflare's
# always-passes TEST key, so a local checkout works without the secret that
# would be needed to verify a real token.
#
# Secret key (server-only, used to verify the token). If unset, verification is
# SKIPPED in dev and HARD-FAILS in production (never silently open) — which
# since the admin login joined means an unset secret in production locks
# /admin out rather than leaving it unguarded. Never commit this one.
# TURNSTILE_SECRET_KEY=
#
# Where a token may have been solved. A genuine token from a copy of the login
# page hosted elsewhere is still genuine, and this is what refuses it. Comma-
# separated; preview deployments need their own host listed.
# Unlike the secret, an empty allowlist SKIPS the check rather than refusing
# everything — an unset variable must not be able to take the admin down on the
# deploy that ships it.
# TURNSTILE_HOSTNAMES=park.fan,www.park.fan

# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# User photo contributions (/[locale]/contribute)
# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Storage: photos are proxied THROUGH our server to a PRIVATE Vercel Blob store
# (one request per file, each downscaled client-side to stay under Vercel's ~4.5 MB
# body limit — the browser never pushes to Blob directly). Submission metadata (the
# moderation queue) is stored as JSON in the same store.
# Provided automatically when you link a Vercel Blob store to the project. The daily
# content-change crawl (/api/cron/content-changes) keeps its dates in the same store.
# BLOB_READ_WRITE_TOKEN=
# Storage driver override. Auto-detects 'vercel-blob' when BLOB_READ_WRITE_TOKEN is
# present, else 'local' (writes to ./.uploads + ./.data — offline dev only). Force with:
# STORAGE_DRIVER=local
# Secret used to HMAC-sign the short-lived upload tickets. Optional — falls back to
# BLOB_READ_WRITE_TOKEN. Set a dedicated value to rotate independently:
# CONTRIBUTE_TICKET_SECRET=

# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Admin write access (/admin/blog-editor, /admin/media)
# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# The blog editor and the media admin do not write to a database — they commit to
# THIS repository and open a draft pull request. Without this token both render
# and let you edit, but saving fails with "Set BLOG_EDITOR_GITHUB_TOKEN …".
#
# Create a FINE-GRAINED PAT (github.com → Settings → Developer settings →
# Personal access tokens → Fine-grained tokens → Generate new token):
#
#   Repository access   Only select repositories → PArns/park.fan
#   Permissions         Contents:      Read and write   (branches, files)
#                       Pull requests: Read and write   (open + update the PR)
#                       Metadata:      Read-only        (added automatically)
#
# Nothing else. A classic PAT works too but needs the whole `repo` scope, which is
# far more access than this needs.
#
# Set it on the deployment (Vercel → Project → Settings → Environment Variables)
# for Production and Preview; put it in .env.local for local admin work. Note that
# fine-grained tokens expire — saving starts failing on the expiry date, so set a
# reminder or pick a long lifetime.
# BLOG_EDITOR_GITHUB_TOKEN=
# Falls back to GITHUB_TOKEN when unset.

# Repository the admin writes to, and the branch its pull requests target.
# Defaults to PArns/park.fan and main.
# GITHUB_REPOSITORY=PArns/park.fan
# BLOG_EDITOR_BASE_BRANCH=main

# The blog editor's "translate" button (/api/admin/blog-editor/translate) calls the
# Anthropic API. Without a key the button answers with an error; nothing else uses it.
# BLOG_EDITOR_ANTHROPIC_API_KEY wins over ANTHROPIC_API_KEY. Server-only, never commit.
# BLOG_EDITOR_ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=
# Model id for the translation. Unset → claude-sonnet-4-5.
# BLOG_EDITOR_TRANSLATE_MODEL=

# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Cron jobs (vercel.json) and on-demand revalidation
# Schedules: docs/development/setup.md#cron-jobs
# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Vercel sends `Authorization: Bearer $CRON_SECRET` with every cron call, and every
# /api/cron/* route checks it. Unset → those routes answer 503 and no cron runs.
# CRON_SECRET=

# Host the prewarm cron (/api/cron/prewarm) requests. Unset → https://park.fan.
# PREWARM_BASE_URL=

# Bearer token for POST /api/revalidate, which the backend calls when data changed.
# Unset → the route answers 503. The API sends the same value as its own
# REVALIDATE_SECRET.
# REVALIDATE_SECRET=

# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Vercel Preview Comments → GitHub PR sync (/api/webhooks/vercel-comments)
# Full setup: docs/development/vercel-comment-sync.md
# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Signing secret shown once when the webhook is created via
# `node scripts/setup-vercel-comment-webhook.mjs`. Used to verify the
# HMAC-SHA1 `x-vercel-signature` header. Unset → the route hard-fails (500),
# it is never silently open. PRODUCTION ONLY — the webhook points at park.fan.
# VERCEL_COMMENT_WEBHOOK_SECRET=

# Fine-grained GitHub PAT with "Contents: read & write" on PArns/park.fan.
# Used only to fire the `vercel-comment` repository_dispatch; the Action itself
# runs with the built-in GITHUB_TOKEN.
# GITHUB_DISPATCH_TOKEN=

# Vercel API token. Optional but recommended: lets the route resolve the git
# branch and preview/production target of a deployment when the comment payload
# does not carry them. Without it, comments whose environment cannot be
# determined are dropped (see VERCEL_COMMENT_SYNC_UNKNOWN).
# VERCEL_API_TOKEN=
# VERCEL_TEAM_ID=

# Repo that receives the dispatch. Defaults to PArns/park.fan.
# VERCEL_COMMENT_SYNC_REPO=PArns/park.fan

# Branch treated as production when Vercel does not send an explicit target.
# Comments on it are ignored. Defaults to "main".
# VERCEL_PRODUCTION_BRANCH=main

# Kill switch — set to "off" to drop every incoming comment webhook.
# VERCEL_COMMENT_SYNC=off

# Escape hatch: sync comments even when the environment could not be determined.
# Off by default so production feedback never lands on an unrelated PR.
# VERCEL_COMMENT_SYNC_UNKNOWN=on

# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Cloudflare purge after a production deploy (/api/webhooks/vercel-deploy)
# Setup: docs/architecture/cdn-caching.md#setup
# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# API token with "Zone → Cache Purge → Purge" on park.fan, that zone's ID, and the
# secret Vercel shows when the `deployment.promoted` webhook is created (checked
# against `x-vercel-signature`). Any of them unset → the route answers 503.
# PRODUCTION ONLY.
# CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN=
# CLOUDFLARE_ZONE_ID=
# VERCEL_DEPLOY_WEBHOOK_SECRET=
